<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Portuguese Congo</h1><p class="st-meta">1885 CE – 1975 CE · capital Cabinda</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Portuguese protectorate of Cabinda, an enclave north of the Congo River whose oil and separatism shape Angolan politics.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Portugal had traded on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-loango">Loango</a> coast for centuries. To secure its claim against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a>, it signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-simulambuco">Treaty of Simulambuco</a> with the princes and chiefs of Cabinda on 1 February 1885, establishing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over territory once ruled by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ngoyo">Kingdom of Ngoyo</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kakongo">Kingdom of Kakongo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a> left Cabinda separated from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola</a> by a strip of land given to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>. It was governed from Cabinda town, at first separately and from the 1920s increasingly as a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">Timber</a>, cocoa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> were exported, and from the 1960s offshore oil discovered by Cabinda Gulf Oil became the enclave's main resource.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Separatists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flec">Front for the Liberation of the Enclave of Cabinda</a> claimed independence, but the 1975 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvor-agreement">Alvor Agreement</a> made Cabinda part of independent <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Angola</a>, where it remains a province with a low-level separatist conflict.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/portuguese-congo.jpg" alt="Portuguese Congo" width="320" height="351"><figcaption>Image: NordNordWest · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cabinda_in_Angola_(2024).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1885 CE – 1975 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cabinda</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese, Kikongo (Ibinda)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cabinda, Portuguese Cabinda</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=portuguese-congo" aria-label="Portuguese Congo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5018 2386 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Portuguese Congo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5338 2642L4773 1044M5338 2642L5366 2764"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4773 1044h0M5366 2764h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5338 2642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5338 2642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cabinda. <a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=portuguese-congo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
